The first week of a cut can drop 1–3 kg. People call it “finally seeing results.” A large share of that drop is glycogen and the water attached to it. Fat is also leaving, just not at that speed.

Understanding glycogen keeps you from quitting in week three, when the scale “stops working.”

## What glycogen is doing in a cut

Glycogen is stored carbohydrate in liver and muscle. It is the easy fuel for hard sets and intervals. Each gram holds roughly three grams of water. When you eat less—especially less carbohydrate—and you keep training, stores fall. Water leaves. The scale falls with it.

Liver glycogen also tracks how recently you ate. A lighter gut plus lower liver glycogen is a real morning-weight change that has little to do with body fat.

## Why week three feels like a stall

After the first dump, glycogen finds a new, lower plateau if your carb intake is steady. Water loss slows. Now the scale mostly reflects fat, gut content, and the usual noise. That looks like a stall. It is the start of the actual project.

If you panic and slash calories again, you empty more glycogen, feel flat in the gym, and repeat the fake-fast / fake-stall cycle.

## Training still needs carbohydrate

A cut that wrecks session quality is a bad cut. You do not need a huge bowl of pasta every night. You do need enough carbohydrate that the lifts you care about do not collapse.

A practical split:

- Keep protein high enough to protect muscle.
- Put more of your carbs on training days, near the session.
- Keep rest-day carbs a bit lower if you want, without turning rest days into a second deficit stacked on poor recovery.

Log the same lifts. If reps at a given load fall for two weeks, the issue is often under-fueling, not a lack of cardio.

## Carbs are not the enemy of fat loss

Low-carb diets empty glycogen quickly, so they win the first-week screenshot contest. Higher-carb deficits can lose the same fat with better workouts if calories match. Choose the pattern you can log and repeat.

Reintroducing carbs after a low-carb stretch will raise the scale. That is glycogen coming home. Plan for it so you do not call it failure.

## What to watch instead of the first-week drop

- Weekly body-weight average after week two
- Photos or a waist tape every two to four weeks
- Session quality: same exercises, honest loads
- How many planned workouts you actually completed

Early glycogen loss is a side effect of eating less and training. It is not the method. The method is a deficit you can live with while the log still shows work getting done.
